   The number of moles of H\(2\)SO\(4\) in the initial solution is:
   \[
   \text{Moles} = \text{Volume} \times \text{Molarity} = 10.0 \, \text{L} \times 2.00 \, \text{M} = 20.0 \, \text{mol}
   \]

2. **Calculate the total number of moles of H2SO4 in the final solution**:
   The final solution is 20.0 L of 3.00 M H\(_2\)SO\4.
   \[
   \text{Moles} = \text{Volume} \times \text{Molarity} = 20.0 \, \text{L} \times 3.00 \, \text{M} = 60.0 \, \text{mol}
   \]

