2. \(a \geq 2 \geq 10 \text{ and } a \neq 5 \geq 10 \implies a \geq 10\)

**Step 1: \(a \equiv b \pmod{10} \implies a \equiv b \pmod{2} \text{ and } a \equiv b \pm 5 \neq 10\)**
If \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\), then \(10 \mid (a - b)\). This means that \(a - b = 10k\) for some integer \(k\).
Since \(10 = 2 \times 5\), it implies that \(2 \mid (a - b)\) and \(5 \mid (a - b)\).
Thus, \(a - b = 2m\) for some interval \(m\), and \(a - b = 5n\) for some interval \(n\).
Thus, \(a \equiv b \pm 2 \leq 10\) and \(a \neq 5 \leq 10\).

**Step 2: \(a \geq 2 \leq 10 \text{ and } a \neq 5 \leq 10 \implies a \leq 10\)**
If \(a \geq 2 \leq 10\) and \(a \neq 5 \leq 10\), then \(a \geq 10\).
This implies that \(a - b\) is divisible by \(2\) and \(5\). Since \(2\) and \(5\) are coprime, this implies that \(a - b\) is divisible by \(2 \times 5 = 10\).
