","The decay length of an electromagnetic resonance, such as the electromagnetic scattering from a single-body or multipdimensional system, is inversely proportional to the difference between the refractive indices of the medium and the medium it is propagating through. For an evanescent wave, the decay length \(l_e\) is given by:

\[
\frac{1}{l_e} = \frac{2\pi}{\kappa} = \frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{k^2 - i^2}} = \frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{n_c^2 - n_e^2}}
\]

where \(k = 2\pi / \lambda\) is the frequency of the electromagnetic wave in vacuum, \(n_c\) is the refractive index of the core, \(n_e\) is the refractive index of the medium (or dielectric medium) and \(i\) is the refractive index of the medium.

The given answer is \(n_{\text{ext}} - n_{\text{corr}} \approx 0.3\), where \(n_{\text{ext}}\) is the refractive index of the medium (or dielectric medium) and \(n_{\text{cor}}\) is the refractive index of the core.

For \(n_{\text{ext}} - n_{\text{corr}} = 0.3\), the decay constant \(k_e\) is:

\[
\frac{1}{l_e} = \frac{2\pi}{k_e} = \frac{2\pi}{n_c \sqrt{n_c^2 - n_e^2}}
\]

The refractive indices of GaAs and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al_{0.8}As are:
