(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The force on the proton due to the electric field is given by \(
F = qE,
\) where \( q \) is the charge of the proton and \( E \) is the strength of the electric field. Substituting the given values:
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}.
\]
Using Newton's second law, \( F = ma \), we can find the acceleration of the proton:
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} \approx 4.79 \times 10^{9} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]
The acceleration of the proton is approximately \( 4.79 \times 10^9 \, \text{m/s}^2 \).

(b) The problem gives the initial velocity \( v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s} \), the final velocity \( v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s} \), and the acceleration \( a = 4.79 \times 10^9 \, \text{m/s}^2 \). We can use the equation:
\[
v = v_0 + at.
\]
Solving for \( t \):
\[
t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 - 0}{4.79 \times 10^9} = \frac{3.5 \times 10^4}{4.79 \times 10^9} \approx 7.32 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{s}.
\]
The time it takes for the proton to reach a speed of \( 35,000 \, \text{m/s} \) is approximately \( 7.32 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{s} \).

