A spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth. Two clocks are on board the ship - one in the cockpit and one in the cargo bay. If the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, what will be the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth? Use the concept of time dilation to calculate the answer.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To solve this problem, we need to find the time dilation experienced by the spaceship's clocks as observed by an stationary observer on Earth. The time dilation formula is:

Δt = Δt₀ √(1 - v²/c²)

where Δt is the time observed by the observer on Earth, Δt₀ is the time observed in the moving moving frame (the spaceship), and v is the relative velocity of the spaceship.

First, we know Δt₀ = 3 years (the time on the cockpit clock as observed by the spaceship's observers). The velocity of the spaceship is v = 0.6c.

Now, we can plug these values into the time dilation equation to find Δt, the time experienced by the spaceship's clocks as observed by an observer on Earth:

Δt = 3 years * √(1 - (0.6c)²/c²)
Δt = 3 years * √(1 - 0.36)
Δt = 3 years * √0.64
Δt = 3 years * 0.8
Δt = 2.4 years

So, the time experienced by the spaceship's clocks (both in the cockpit and the logo) as observed by an observer on Earth is 2.4 years.

The time on the ship's cocopping clock is 3 years. The time on the cargo bay clock is the same as the cocopping one, as both are on the same spaceship. Therefore, the time on the cargo bay clock when it is compressed to a clock on Earth is 2.4 years.
