How would you expect the yield strength and percent elongation of an aluminum alloyed with 4% copper (containing hard intermetallic CuAl$_2$ particles due to thermal processing) to differ from those of pure aluminum? Explain the reasons for these differences.
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The yield strength of the aluminum alloyed with 4% copper would be higher than that of pure aluminum due to the presence of hard intermetallic CuAl$_2$ particles. These particles act as obstacles to dislocation motion, making it more difficult for dislocations to move and thus increasing the stress required for yielding (yield strength).  

The percent elongation of the alloy would be lower than that of pure aluminum because the CuAl$_2$ particles hinder dislocation movement, reducing the material's ability to deform plastically before fracture. This results in decreased ductility.  

In summary:  
- Yield strength: $\boxed{\text{higher}}$ due to obstacles (CuAl$_2$ particles) impeding dislocation motion.  
- Percent elongation: $\boxed{\text{lower}}$ due to reduced plastic deformation capability.
</GROUND_TRUTH>

