","To calculate the work done by the gas during the isothermal expansion, we use the formula for work done in an isothermal process:

\[
W = -NRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right)
\]

where:
- \( W = \text{work done} \)
- \( N = \text{number of moles} \)
- \( R = 8.314 \text{ J/mol·K} = 0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K} \) (using L-atm as the work pressure)
- \( T = \text{temperature} \)
- \( V_1 = \text{initial volume} \)
- \( V_2 = \text{final volume} \)

First, we need to find the temperature \( T \). We can use the ideal gas law:

\[
PV = nRT
\]

Substituting the initial conditions:
\[
(2 \text{ atm})(4 \text{ L}) = (2 \text{ mol})(0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K}) T
\]
\[
8 = 0.1642T
\]
\[
T = \frac{8}{0.1642} \approx 48.9 \text{ K}
\]

Now, we can calculate the work done:
\[
W = -nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right) = -(2 \text{ mol})(0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K})(48.9 \text{ K}) \ln \left( \frac{8 \text{ L}}{4 \text{ L}} \right)
\]
\[
W = -(2 \times 0.0821 \times 48.9) \ln(2) = -1.65 \times 1.386 \text{ L·atm}
\]
\[
W = -1.65 \times 0.693 \text{ L·atm} \approx -1.14 \text{ L·atm}
\]

To convert the work from L-atm to joules, we use the conversion factor \( 1 \text{ L·atm} = 101.3 \text{ J} \):
\[
W = -1.14 \text{ L·atm} \times 101.3 \text{ J/L·atm} \approx -115.5 \text{ J}
\]

Thus, the work done by the gas during the expansion process is \(\boxed{-115.5 \text{ J}}\).",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
